turns.content:
The arithmetic you stated is correct: $5,200 − $4,200 = $1,000. That is a cash-flow calculation if you pay his share for three months. It is not proof that you must pay it, that Julian is released, or that a missed payment will be reported on both names.

What is established from what you have shown: Clause 14, as quoted, does not treat a key drop or a text as a release. What is not established: whether management will still treat Julian as jointly and severally liable, how they will bill remaining rent, or how any nonpayment would be reported.

Do not move the $4,200 out of savings on the basis of the note and text. Call management first and get written answers to:

- whether a Release and Assignment Addendum for Julian has been executed;
- if not, whether they still list him as jointly and severally liable;
- the exact amount they will demand, from whom, and on what dates;
- whether they will accept a replacement resident under the addendum the clause names.

Until those answers exist, paying his share is a choice to keep the account current, not a legal conclusion that the obligation is yours alone. Paying preserves the lease account and leaves about $1,000 in the savings figure you reported; not paying risks whatever collection or reporting management actually uses. Either path is reversible only to the extent management and Julian later put something in writing.

Next step: make that call, quote Clause 14, and ask for the answers in writing before you spend the $4,200. If the remaining rent is large relative to the $1,000 buffer, have a lawyer or tenant clinic read the full lease after you have management’s written position.
